Extension API: let an injected pointer position survive until the
input is consumed.

STATUS: NEEDED for any injected click whose handler reads the
POINTER rather than the event. Blocks the colony summary's
population move today. This is `doc/orion2re_open_fixes.md` item 3,
second half.

    APPLIED LOCALLY on 4 September 2026, by decision, to the
    orion2re working tree at ~/orion2re (engine 1.60.0, git
    cf4d9617). Not a silent change: it is recorded here and under
    item 3 of doc/orion2re_open_fixes.md, and it comes back off
    with one line —

        cd ~/orion2re && patch -R -p1 < \
            ~/orionlayerv3/doc/ext_inject_pointer.patch

    A locally patched engine is a version that exists nowhere
    else, so it either shows up at the next update or disappears
    silently. That is the cost of running it, and the reason the
    patch is also the request to Joes.

    THE HUNKS BELOW ARE GENERATED, not hand-written: the edits
    were made and then diffed, so the line numbers are the file's
    own. An earlier hand-written version of this patch would not
    have applied.

WHAT BREAKS TODAY

    COLSUM::Get_Selected_Pop_ (colsum.cpp:1006) resolves which pop
    icon was clicked from mouse::Pointer_X_(), not from the click
    point. An injected click reaches the right FIELD but selects no
    icon, so Get_Cluster_ is never called and the click does nothing
    at all. Measured 4 September 2026, twice, at a 640x480 window
    where the coordinate mapping is the identity — so the mapping is
    not the cause.

WHY THE OBVIOUS FIX IS NOT THE FIX

    "Carry the position with the event" is already how the engine
    works and is not the missing piece:

        Enqueue_Mouse_Input_Event_(x, y, buttons)   platform.cpp:132
            carries the position in the queued event
        mouse::Pointer_X_()                        mouse.cpp:51
            calls Pump_Game_Input_Queue_() FIRST, then reads
        User_Mouse_Handler_                        mouse.cpp:237
            sets the logical position FROM the event

    So the design is already right. What defeats it is
    Sync_Mouse_State_From_SDL_ (platform.cpp:825), which runs every
    frame while the window has focus and does two things:

        Set_Present_Mouse_Position_(real_x, real_y)   :846
        Enqueue_Mouse_Input_Event_(real_x, real_y, …) :852
            when the real position differs from the logical one

    and the second one is the sharp edge, because
    Enqueue_Mouse_Input_Event_ COALESCES: if the last queued event is
    a mouse event with the same button state, it overwrites that
    event's coordinates rather than appending (platform.cpp:138-147).
    The injected button-up carries buttons = 0; so does the sync's
    position event. The sync therefore rewrites the injected
    release's coordinates to wherever the physical mouse is.

    When the queue is finally pumped, the press is handled at the
    injected point — which is why the right field is reached — and
    the release then moves the logical pointer to the real mouse.
    By the time Get_Selected_Pop_ asks, the pointer has already
    walked away from the click.

THE FIX

    Suppress the sync while injected input is still unconsumed.
    Deterministic rather than timed: the flag is set when a click is
    injected and cleared when the input queue drains, so the
    suppression lasts exactly as long as the injected events are in
    flight and not one frame longer.

PRICE

    While suppressed, the game does not see real mouse movement or
    real button changes. That window is the one or two frames between
    injecting and pumping. It cannot leak: the clear is in
    Pump_Game_Input_Queue_, which is called by Pointer_X_(),
    Pointer_Y_() and the main loop, so any code that asks about the
    mouse at all ends the suppression.

    A player moving the physical mouse during those frames loses that
    movement. On a hidden game window there is no physical mouse over
    it to lose.

    The alternative — tagging queued events with their origin and
    refusing to coalesce across origins — is a bigger change to a
    hot path, and it would still leave Set_Present_Mouse_Position_ at
    :846 overwriting the position outright.

NOT A SOLUTION, AND DELIBERATELY NOT USED
    Sync_Mouse_State_From_SDL_ already returns early when
    g_window_focus_state == 0 (platform.cpp:826), so an injected
    pointer survives while the window is unfocused. Nothing on the
    wire reports focus, so a client cannot know it is in that state,
    and "inject only while unfocused" would be behaviour depending on
    something invisible. It explains why an injected click can appear
    to work intermittently; it is not a way to live with the bug.

--- a/src/ext/ext_api.h
+++ b/src/ext/ext_api.h
@@ -24,4 +24,10 @@
 /// Set by ProcessInput(), consumed by Get_Input_() early return.
 extern int16_t g_pending_field;
 
+/// True while an injected mouse event is queued and not yet
+/// pumped. Sync_Mouse_State_From_SDL_ stands aside while this is
+/// set, so the injected position survives to whoever reads
+/// mouse::Pointer_X_(). Cleared by Pump_Game_Input_Queue_().
+extern bool g_injected_mouse_pending;
+
 } // namespace ext
--- a/src/ext/ext_api.cpp
+++ b/src/ext/ext_api.cpp
@@ -13,6 +13,7 @@
 
 bool g_hide_window = false;
 int16_t g_pending_field = 0;
+bool g_injected_mouse_pending = false;
 
 static Server g_server;
 static int16_t g_last_screen = -99;
@@ -206,6 +207,13 @@
             // Must send both DOWN and UP — the game's input loop
             // waits for button release before returning.
             {
+                // Hold the real-mouse sync off until these are
+                // consumed. Without it, Enqueue_Mouse_Input_Event_'s
+                // coalescing rewrites the injected RELEASE to the
+                // physical mouse position, and the pointer has walked
+                // away before the game reads it.
+                g_injected_mouse_pending = true;
+
                 SDL_Event down{};
                 down.type = SDL_EVENT_MOUSE_BUTTON_DOWN;
                 down.button.button = SDL_BUTTON_LEFT;
--- a/src/game/platform.cpp
+++ b/src/game/platform.cpp
@@ -827,6 +827,19 @@
 				return;
 			}
 
+#ifdef ORION2RE_EXT
+			// An injected click already carries its position in the
+			// queued event and Pointer_X_() pumps before reading, so
+			// the design is sound. What breaks it is this function:
+			// it overwrites the position, and its enqueue coalesces
+			// over the injected release (same button state), pulling
+			// the pointer back to the physical mouse. Stand aside
+			// until the queue has been pumped.
+			if (ext::g_injected_mouse_pending) {
+				return;
+			}
+#endif
+
 			float mouse_x = 0.0f;
 			float mouse_y = 0.0f;
 			const SDL_MouseButtonFlags sdl_buttons = SDL_GetMouseState(&mouse_x, &mouse_y);
@@ -1490,6 +1503,15 @@
                 Queue_Key_Code_(event.key_code, event.scan_code);
             }
         }
+
+#ifdef ORION2RE_EXT
+        // The injected events have been handled, so the real mouse may
+        // speak again. Cleared here rather than after a frame count:
+        // this runs from Pointer_X_(), Pointer_Y_() and the main loop,
+        // so the suppression ends the moment anything asks about the
+        // mouse, and it cannot outlive the events it protects.
+        ext::g_injected_mouse_pending = false;
+#endif
     }
 
